#!/usr/bin/python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
rhodep-gnss-daemon - publish the modem's cell-id location as standard NMEA.

Motorola rhodep (SM6375). The QMI LOC service on qrtr://0 is alive and a
location session runs happily -- but ONLY in operation mode 'cellid'. Starting a
session in standalone/default/msa/msb power-cycles the SoC inside 100 ms,
reproducibly. See docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/GNSS-SM6375.md and
scripts/rhodep-gnss-test.py for how that was bisected.

So this daemon:

  * opens qrtr://0, allocates ONE QmiClientLoc (all of the below has to happen
    from the same client: the session belongs to the client that created it),
  * registers for position-report / nmea / gnss-sv-info / engine-state,
  * sets operation mode to cellid AND WAITS FOR THE set-operation-mode
    INDICATION TO CONFIRM IT, then reads the mode back with get-operation-mode
    and refuses to go any further unless the modem itself says CELLID,
  * only then starts the session,
  * turns each QMI position report into real $GPGGA/$GPRMC/$GPGSA sentences at
    1 Hz (the modem's own NMEA in cellid mode is just $PSTIS, which is useless
    to gpsd/geoclue), and fans them out to a UNIX socket and optionally a TCP
    port.

The mode check is the whole safety story. A set_operation_mode that is merely
"accepted" is not enough: the LOC service acks the request and reports the real
outcome in an indication, and a mode that never got applied leaves the engine in
standalone -- which reboots the phone. Hence: ack + indication + readback, three
gates, and no session if any of them is not CELLID.

  --i-know-this-reboots-the-phone  is the only way to select another mode. It
    exists so a future kernel fix can be tested without editing this file. Do not
    use it on hardware you want to stay up.

Location sources (--source)
---------------------------

  qmi-cellid  the above: a QMI LOC session pinned to cellid. Needs a modem that
              has a serving cell it can resolve itself, which in practice needs
              a provisioned SIM. This is the only source that opens the QMI LOC
              service.
  wifi        no modem at all. Scans the WiFi neighbourhood and asks an
              Ichnaea-compatible geolocation service (BeaconDB, Positon, ...)
              where those BSSIDs are. Touches nothing on the modem side, so it
              cannot trip the GNSS reset bug even in principle.
  cell        reads the identity of the GERAN/UMTS/LTE cells the modem can hear
              -- QMI NAS get-cell-location-info, a read -- and resolves those
              identities to a position, first against a local OpenCelliD
              extract and then against the same geolocation services. This
              works with an unprovisioned SIM and a modem that never registers,
              because a modem still measures and decodes the broadcast identity
              of the cells around it while it is searching. It does not open the
              QMI LOC service and does not start a location session.
  auto        (default) qmi-cellid when the modem is actually registered on a
              network; otherwise wifi and cell together, preferring wifi.
              Registration is read from ModemManager over D-Bus, which is a
              plain property read; the LOC service is not opened unless that
              check passes.

Why NAS reads are safe and LOC is not. The thing that power-cycles this SoC is
the GNSS measurement engine being started, which happens when QMI_LOC_START is
sent in an operation mode that involves the satellite receiver. QMI NAS is the
network-access service: get-cell-location-info and get-serving-system report
what the radio has already measured while looking for a network. They start
nothing, and they have been run on this phone hundreds of times without an
uptime reset. This daemon therefore allocates a NAS client for --source=cell
and for --source=auto, and allocates a LOC client only on the qmi-cellid path.
The service ids are not interchangeable anywhere in this file; see CellReader.

Why the WiFi and cell sources talk to the geolocation service themselves rather
than asking geoclue: geoclue is configured on this phone to take its position
from *this daemon's* NMEA socket (network-nmea source). Sourcing our position
back out of geoclue would be a loop. So the daemon does its own scan-and-query
and geoclue stays a pure consumer.

Usage:
  rhodep-gnss-daemon [--source auto|wifi|cell|qmi-cellid] [--socket PATH]
                     [--tcp PORT] [--rate HZ] [-v]

Requires: python3-gi. --source=qmi-cellid/cell/auto additionally want
gir1.2-qmi-1.0 (or, failing that, the qmicli binary from libqmi-utils);
--source=wifi wants either NetworkManager (preferred, scans without root and
without forcing the radio off-channel) or iw. The offline cell database is
built by rhodep-cell-db and is optional.
Modem must be out of low power for the QMI paths:
  qmicli -d qrtr://0 --dms-set-operating-mode=online
"""

